-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       Comando Quatro
  Publisher   Zigurat / GameSoft, 1989
  Version     1.0

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch:

  File        Comando Quatro (1989)(Zigurat)(ES).z80
  MD5         47ff99afedc9f0af0030b63d1fa4d098

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures are drawn on the Next's own graphics layers, so they keep their
  own colours and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is
  unchanged.

  Pace        original (-0.27% measured in JNext 1.1.104, against the original
              .z80 on a 48K machine, game state in lockstep)

  Changes     1.0: figures in their own colours on a double-buffered Layer 2,
              in the game's own plot order, over the original bitmap; scenery
              in its own attributes; the plot, restore and copy run whole at
              28 MHz in pace windows; the original loading screen; Sinclair's
              48K ROM as the whole alt ROM (NextZXOS 26.11 safe); the game's
              speed restored after every hook; two tested cheats

  Note        Keys: any key at the picture; menu 1 keyboard, 2 Kempston, 3
              define keys. Q up, A down, O left, P right, Space fire, 1 to 4
              pick a commando, C the game's colour switch, 0 pause, 8 abort.
              The original look with the clash gone: each figure's ink is
              drawn on Layer 2 over the original bitmap, in the game's own
              order, and scenery keeps its own colours where the original
              painted cells in the figure's ink. The menu and a few messages
              are in Spanish; no text matters in play. The 48K game with its
              beeper sound; fits a 1MB Next. Known issues: with the C switch
              off, figures the game then leaves unpainted stay in the cell's
              ink, as in the original; pace in JNext against the original, 6
              runs of 800 updates over the four zones, state in lockstep:
              whole runs -0.47 to -0.27%, quiet thirds -0.41 to -0.11%, busy
              thirds -0.74 to -0.55%, slowest 20 updates up to +1.09%.
              Missing: Enhanced (no source: potential rated only); no English
              set; natural play past the first rooms of each zone; hardware
              untested.

-- MENU ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   1  Infinite energy
   2  Infinite time

  The archive's POKEs for this release, each tested in the simulator, same
  keys with and without it, 3 seeds x 12,000 frames: Infinite Energy (no
  commando loses a life; 8 to 13 lost without) and Infinite Time (the time bar
  never moves, against all 64 positions without). Both offered.

